"hadiths[182]{hadithnumber,text}:"
4519,"Ibn 'Aun reported:I wrote to Nafi' inquiring from him whether it was necessary to extend (to the disbelievers) an invitation to accept (Islam) before meeting them in fight. He wrote (in reply) to me that it was necessary in the early days of Islam. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) made a raid upon Banu Mustaliq while they were unaware and their cattle were having a drink at the water. He killed those who fought and imprisoned others. On that very day"
4520,"This hadith has been narrated on the authority of Ibn 'Aun and the name of Juwairiya bint al-Harith was mentioned beyond any doubt"
4521,"It has been reported from Sulaiman b. Buraida through his father that when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) appointed anyone as leader of an army or detachment he would especially exhort him to fear Allah and to be good to the Muslims who were with him. He would say:Fight in the name of Allah and in the way of Allah. Fight against those who disbelieve in Allah. Make a holy war"
4522,"It has been reported from Sulaiman b. Buraida through his father that when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) appointed anyone as leader of an army or detachment he would especially exhort him to fear Allah and to be good to the Muslims who were with him. He would say:Fight in the name of Allah and in the way of Allah. Fight against those who disbelieve in Allah. Make a holy war"
4523,"Sulaiman b. Buraida repotted on the authority of his father that when Allah's Messenger (ﷺ) sent an Amir with a detachment he called him and advised him. The rest of the hadith is the same"
4524,"This hadith has been transmitted on the authority of Shu'ba"
4525,"It has been narrated on the authority of Abu Masa that when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) deputed any of his Companions on a mission"
4526,"It has also been narrated by Sa'd b. Abu Burda through his father through his grandfather that the Prophet of Allah (ﷺ) sent him and Mu'adh (on a mission) to the Yemen"
4527,"This hadith has been transmitted on the authority of Buraida but for the last two words"
4528,"The Messenger of Allah (may peace he upon him) has been reported by Anas b. Malik to have said:Show leniency; do not be hard; give solace and do not create aversion"
4529,"It has been narrated on the authority of Ibn 'Umar that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:When Allah will gather together"
4530,"This hadith has been narrated on the authority of Ibn Umar through some other Chains of transmitters"
4531,"This hadith has been narrated by another chain of transmitters on the authority of the same narrator"
4532,"Ibn Umar reported that he heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saying:There will be a flag for every perfidious person on the Day of Judgment"
4533,"Abdullah reported Allah's Prophet (ﷺ) as saying:There will be a flag for every perfidious person on the Day of Judgment"
4534,"This hadith has been narrated on the authority of Shu'ba with a slight variation of wording"
4535,"It has been narrated on the authority of Abdullah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:There will be for every perfidious person on the Day of Judgment a flag by which he will be recognised. It will be announced: Here is the breach of faith of so and so"
4536,"Anas reported Allah's Messenger (ﷺ) having said this:There would be a flag for every perfidious person on the Day of Judgment by which he will be recognised"
4537,"It is narrated on the authority of Abu Sa'id that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:On the Day of Judgment there will be a flag fixed behind the buttocks of every person guilty of the breach of faith"
4538,"It is narrated on the authority of Abu Sa'id that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:On the Day of Judgment there will be a flag for every person guilty of the breach of faith. It will be raised in proportion to the extent of his guilt; and there is no guilt of treachery more serious than the one committed by the ruler of men"
4539,"It is narrated on the authority of Jabir that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:War is a stratagem"
4540,"This hadith has also been narrated on the authority of Abu Huraira"
4541,"It has been narrated on the authority of Abu Huraira that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:Do not desire an encounter with the enemy; but when you encounter them"
4542,"It is narrated by Abu Nadr that he learnt from a letter sent by a man from the Aslam tribe"
4543,"It is narrated on the authority of Ibn Abu Aufa that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) cursed the tribes (who had marched upon Medina with a combined force in 5 H) and said:O Allah"
4544,"This hadith has been transmitted on the authority of Ibn Abu Aufa with a slight variation of words"
4545,"This hadith has been narrated on the authority of Ibn 'Uyaina through another chain of transmitters (who added the words)"""" the Disperser of clouds"""" in his narration"
4546,"It is narrated on the authority of Anas that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said on the day of the Battle of Uhud:O Allah"
4547,"It is narrated on the authority of 'Abdullah that a woman was found killed in one of the battles fought by the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). He disapproved of the killing of women and children"
4548,"It is narrated by Ibn 'Umar that a woman was found killed in one of these battles; so the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) forbade the killing of women and children"
4549,"It is reported on the authority of Sa'b b. Jaththama that the Prophet of Allah (ﷺ)"
4550,"It is narrated by Sa'b b. Jaththama that he said (to the Holy Prophet):Messenger of Allah"
4551,"Sa'b b. Jaththama has narrated that the Prophet (ﷺ) asked:What about the children of polytheists killed by the cavalry during the night raid? He said: They are from them"
4552,"It is narrated on the authority of 'Abdullah that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) ordered the date-palms of Banu Nadir to be burnt and cut. These palms were at Buwaira. Qutaibah and Ibn Rumh in their versions of the tradition have added:So Allah"
4553,"It is narrated on the authority of Ibn Umar that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) caused the date-palms of Banu Nadir to be cut down and burnt. It is in this connection that Hassan (the poet) said:It was easy for the nobles of Quraish to burn Buwaira whose sparks were flying in all directions"
4554,"Abdullah b. Umar reported that Allah's Apostle (ﷺ) burnt the date-palms of Banu Nadir"
4555,"It has been narrated by Abu Huraira that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:One of the Prophets made a holy war. He said to his followers: One who has married a woman and wants to consummate to his marriage but has not yet done so; another who has built a house but has not yet erected its roof; and another who has bought goats and pregnantshe-camels and is waiting for their offspring-will not accommpany me. So he marched on and approached a village at or about the time of the Asr prayers. He said to the sun: Thou art subserviant (to Allah) and so am I. O Allah"
4556,"A hadith has been narrated by Mus'ab b. Sa'd who heard it from his father as saying:My father took a sword from Khums and brought it to the Prophet (ﷺ) and said: Grant it to me. He refused. At this Allah revealed (the Qur'anic verse):"""" They ask thee concerning the spoils of war. Say: The spoils of war are for Allah and the Apostle"""" (viii)"
4557,"A hadith has been narrated by Mus'ab b. Sa'd who heard it from his father as saying:"""" Four verses of the Qur'an have been revealed about me. I found a sword (among the spoils of war). It was brought to the Prophet (ﷺ). He (my father) said: Messenger of Allah"
4558,"It has been narrated on the authority of Ibn Umar that the Prophet (ﷺ) sent an expedition to Najd and I was among the troops. They got a large number of camels as a booty. Eleven or twelve camels fell to the lot of every fighter and each of them also got one extra camel"
4559,"Ibn 'Umar reported that Allah's Messenger (ﷺ) sent an expedition to Najd and Ibn Umar was also among the troops"
4560,"It has been narrated by Ibn 'Umar that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent an expedition to Najd"
4561,"This hadith has been narrated on the authority of 'Ubaidullah with the same chain of transmitters"
4562,"Ibn Aun said:I wrote to Nafi' asking him about Nafl (spoils of war) and be wrote to me that Ibn 'Umar was among that expedition. (The rest of the hadith is the same)"
4563,"A hadith has been narrated by Salim who learnt it from his father and said:The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) gave us an extra (camel) besides our share of Khums; (and in this extra share) I got a Sharif (and a Sharif is a big old camel)"
4564,"Ibn Shihab reported:It reached me through Ibn Umar that Allah's Messenger (ﷺ) gave a share of spoils to the troop. The rest of the hadith is the same"
4565,"It has been narrated on the authority of Abdullah b. 'Umar that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to give (from the spoils of war) to small troops seat on expeditions something more than the due share of each fighter in a large force. And Khums (one-fifth of the total spoils) was to be reserved (for Allah and His Apostle) in all cases"
4566,"Abu Muammad al-Ansari, who was the close companion of Abu Qatada. narrated the hadith (which follows)."
4567,"Abu Muhammad, the freed slave of Abu Qatada reported on the authority of Abu Qatda and narrated the hadith."
4568,"Abu Qatada reported:We accompanied the Messenger of Allah (my peace be upon him) on an expedition in the year of the Battle of Hunain. When we encountered the enemy"
4569,"It has been narrated on the authority of 'Abd al-Rahman b. Auf who said:While I was standing in the battle array on the Day of Badr"
4570,"Auf b. Malik has narrated that a man from the Himyar tribe killed an enemy and wanted to take the booty. Khalid b. Walid"
4571,"It has been narrated on the authority of Auf b. Malik al-Ashja'i who said:I joined the expedition that marched under Zaid b. Haritha to Muta"
4572,"It has been reported by Salama b. al-Akwa':We fought the Battle of Hawazin along with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). (One day) when we were having our breakfast with the Messenger of Allah (may peace he upon him)"
4573,"It has been narrated on the authority of Salama (b. al-Akwa') who said:We fought against the Fazara"
4574,"It has been narrated on the authority of Abu Huraira that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:If you come to a township (which has surrendered without a formal war) and stay therein"
4575,"It has been narrated on the authority of Umar, who said:
The properties abandoned by Banu Nadir were the ones which Allah bestowed upon His Apostle for which no expedition was undertaken either with cavalry or camelry. These properties were particularly meant for the Prophet ( صلی اللہ علیہ وسلم ). He would meet the annual expenditure of his family from the income thereof, and would spend what remained for purchasing horses and weapons as preparation for Jihad."
4576,"This hadith has been narrated on the authority of Zuhri"
4577,"It is reported by Zuhri that this tradition was narrated to him by Malik b. Aus who said:Umar b. al-Khattab sent for me and I came to him when the day had advanced. I found him in his house sitting on his bare bed-stead"
4578,"The same hadith has been narrated by a different chain of transmitters with a slight variation in wording:'Umar b. al-Khattab sent for me and said: Some families from your tribe have come to me (then follows the foregoing hadith) by Malik with the difference that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) would spend on his family for a year. And sometimes Ma'mar said: He would retain sustenance for his family for a year"
4579,"It is narrated on the authority of 'A'isha who said:When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) passed away"
4580,"Muhammad ibn Rafi' narrated to me, Hujayn informed us, Layth narrated to us, from 'Uqayl, from Ibn Shihab, from 'Urwah ibn az-Zubayr, from 'Aishah, that she informed him that Fatimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (may peace be upon him), sent word to Abu Bakr as-Siddiq asking him for her inheritance from the Messenger of Allah (may peace be upon him), from what Allah had granted him as spoils in Medina, and Fadak, and what remained of the khumus (fifth) of Khaybar. Abu Bakr said: ""The Messenger of Allah (may peace be upon him) said: 'We are not inherited from; whatever we leave is charity.' The family of Muhammad (may peace be upon him) only eat from this wealth. And by Allah, I will not change anything of the charity of the Messenger of Allah (may peace be upon him) from the state it was in during the time of the Messenger of Allah (may peace be upon him), and I will certainly deal with it as the Messenger of Allah (may peace be upon him) dealt with it."" So Abu Bakr refused to give Fatimah anything. Fatimah was displeased with Abu Bakr because of this, so she avoided him and did not speak to him until she died. She lived six months after the Messenger of Allah (may peace be upon him). When she died, her husband 'Ali ibn Abi Talib buried her at night and did not inform Abu Bakr of it, and 'Ali led the funeral prayer over her. During Fatimah's lifetime, 'Ali had a certain standing among the people, but when she died, the people's faces turned away from him, so 'Ali sought reconciliation with Abu Bakr and to give him his pledge of allegiance, for he had not given the pledge during those months. He sent word to Abu Bakr saying: ""Come to us, and let no one come with you,"" out of dislike for the presence of 'Umar ibn al-Khattab. 'Umar said to Abu Bakr: ""By Allah, do not go in to them alone."" Abu Bakr said: ""What could they possibly do to me? By Allah, I will indeed go to them."" So Abu Bakr went in to them. 'Ali ibn Abi Talib recited the shahadah (testimony of faith), then said: ""We know, O Abu Bakr, your excellence and what Allah has given you, and we do not begrudge you any good that Allah has brought to you. But you acted alone in this matter, while we thought we had a right due to our kinship with the Messenger of Allah (may peace be upon him)."" He continued speaking to Abu Bakr until Abu Bakr's eyes overflowed with tears. When Abu Bakr spoke, he said: ""By the One in Whose hand is my soul, the kinship of the Messenger of Allah (may peace be upon him) is dearer to me to maintain than my own kinship. As for what occurred between me and you concerning this wealth, I did not fall short of the truth in it, and I did not leave anything that I saw the Messenger of Allah (may peace be upon him) do concerning it except that I did it."" 'Ali said to Abu Bakr: ""Your appointment is this afternoon for the pledge of allegiance."" So when Abu Bakr had prayed the noon prayer, he ascended the pulpit and recited the shahadah, and mentioned the matter of 'Ali and his delay in giving the pledge, and the excuse he had offered for it, then he sought forgiveness. Then 'Ali ibn Abi Talib recited the shahadah, and he extolled the right of Abu Bakr and said that what had made him act as he did was not out of envy of Abu Bakr, nor a denial of that which Allah had favored him with, ""but we thought we had a share in this matter, and it was decided without consulting us, and we were displeased about it in ourselves."" The Muslims were pleased with this and said: ""You have done well."" And the Muslims drew close to 'Ali when he returned to what was right and proper."
4581,"It has been narrated on the authority of 'A'isha that Fatima and 'Abbas approached Abu Bakr"
4582,"Ibn Numair narrated to us: Ya'qub bin Ibrahim narrated to us: My father narrated to us. Another chain: Zuhair bin Harb and Al-Hasan bin Ali Al-Hulwani narrated to us, they said: Ya'qub, who is Ibn Ibrahim, narrated to us: My father narrated to us, from Salih, from Ibn Shihab: Urwah bin Az-Zubair informed me that A'ishah, the wife of the Prophet (may Allah bless him and grant him peace), informed him that Fatimah, the daughter of the Messenger of Allah (may Allah bless him and grant him peace), asked Abu Bakr, after the death of the Messenger of Allah (may Allah bless him and grant him peace), to give her her inheritance from what the Messenger of Allah (may Allah bless him and grant him peace) had left of what Allah had bestowed upon him as spoils. Abu Bakr said to her: The Messenger of Allah (may Allah bless him and grant him peace) said: ""We are not inherited from; whatever we leave is charity."" She (A'ishah) said: Fatimah lived for six months after the Messenger of Allah (may Allah bless him and grant him peace), and Fatimah used to ask Abu Bakr for her share of what the Messenger of Allah (may Allah bless him and grant him peace) had left from Khaibar, Fadak, and his charitable land in Madinah, but Abu Bakr refused to give her that, and said: I will not leave anything that the Messenger of Allah (may Allah bless him and grant him peace) used to do, but that I will do it, for I fear that if I neglect any part of his command, I will go astray. As for his charitable land in Madinah, Umar gave it to Ali and Abbas, and Ali took full control of it. As for Khaibar and Fadak, Umar kept them and said: These two are the charity of the Messenger of Allah (may Allah bless him and grant him peace), which were for his needs that arose and his contingencies, and their affairs are to be managed by whoever is in charge of authority. She (A'ishah) said: And so they have remained thus until today."
4583,"It has been narrated on the authority of Abu Huraira that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:My heirs cannot share even a dinar (from my legacy) ; what I leave behind after paving maintenance allowance to my wives and remuneration to my manager is (to go in) charity"
4584,"A similar hadith has been narrated on the authority of Abu Zinad through a different chain of transmitters"
4585,"It his been narrated on the authority of Abu Huraira that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:"""" We do not have any heirs; what we leave behind is a charitable endowment"
4586,"It has been narrated on the authority of Ibn Umar that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) allowed two shares from the spoils to the horseman and one share to the footman"
4587,"The same tradition has been narrated on the authority of Ubaidullah by a different chain of transmitters who do not mention the words:"""" from the booty"
4588,"Hannad ibn al-Sari narrated to us, [saying]: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Ikrimah ibn Ammar, [who said]: Simak al-Hanafi narrated to me, [who said]: I heard Ibn Abbas say: Umar ibn al-Khattab narrated to me, saying: When it was the day of Badr... [continuing the chain] And Zuhayr ibn Harb narrated to us - and the wording is his - Umar ibn Yunus al-Hanafi narrated to us, Ikrimah ibn Ammar narrated to us, Abu Zumayl - who is Simak al-Hanafi - narrated to me, Abdullah ibn Abbas narrated to me, saying: Umar ibn al-Khattab narrated to me, saying:

""When it was the day of Badr, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) looked at the polytheists, and they numbered one thousand, while his companions numbered three hundred and nineteen men. The Prophet of Allah (ﷺ) faced the qiblah, then stretched out his hands and began to call upon his Lord: 'O Allah, fulfill for me what You have promised me. O Allah, grant me what You have promised me. O Allah, if You destroy this band of the people of Islam, You will not be worshipped on earth.' He continued calling upon his Lord, stretching out his hands, facing the qiblah, until his cloak fell from his shoulders. Abu Bakr came to him, took his cloak, and put it back on his shoulders, then embraced him from behind and said: 'O Prophet of Allah, your imploring of your Lord is enough for you; He will surely fulfill for you what He has promised you.' Then Allah, Mighty and Sublime, revealed: 'When you sought help of your Lord, and He answered you, ""I will reinforce you with a thousand of the angels, following one another.""' [Quran 8:9] So Allah reinforced him with the angels.""

Abu Zumayl said: Ibn Abbas narrated to me, saying: ""While a man from the Muslims was that day pursuing a man from the polytheists ahead of him, he suddenly heard the crack of a whip above him, and the voice of a horseman saying: 'Forward, Hayzum!' He looked at the polytheist ahead of him, and he fell down on his back. He looked at him, and found that his nose had been struck and his face slashed as if by a whip-stroke, and it had all turned green. The Ansari man came and related that to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and he said: 'You have spoken the truth; that was from the reinforcement of the third heaven.' They killed seventy that day and took seventy captive.""

Abu Zumayl said: Ibn Abbas said: ""When they had taken the captives, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said to Abu Bakr and Umar: 'What do you think about these captives?' Abu Bakr said: 'O Prophet of Allah, they are cousins and kinsmen; I think you should take ransom from them, which would be a strength for us against the disbelievers, and perhaps Allah will guide them to Islam.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'What do you think, O son of al-Khattab?' I said: 'No, by Allah, O Messenger of Allah, I do not hold the view that Abu Bakr holds. Rather, I think you should hand them over to us so that we may strike off their necks - hand over Aqil to Ali so that he may strike off his neck, and hand over so-and-so, a relative of Umar, to me so that I may strike off his neck. For these are the leaders of disbelief and its chiefs.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) preferred what Abu Bakr had said, and did not prefer what I had said. The next day, when I came, I found the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and Abu Bakr sitting and weeping. I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, tell me why you and your companion are weeping. If I find cause to weep, I will weep, and if I do not find cause to weep, I will weep out of sympathy for your weeping.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'I am weeping because of what your companions presented to me regarding taking ransom. Indeed, their punishment was shown to me, as near to me as this tree' - a tree near the Prophet of Allah (ﷺ). Then Allah, Mighty and Sublime, revealed: 'It is not for a prophet to have captives until he inflicts a massacre upon [his enemy] in the land...' up to His saying: 'so eat of what you have taken as spoils of war, [as being] lawful and good...' [Quran 8:67-69] So Allah made the spoils of war lawful for them."""
4589,"It has been narrated on the authority of Abu Huraira who said:The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent some horsemen to Najd. They captured a man. He was from the tribe of Banu Hanifa and was called Thumama b. Uthal. He was the chief of the people of Yamama. People bound him with one of the pillars of the mosque. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came out to (see) him. He said: O Thumama"
4590,"The same tradition has been narrated by a different chain of transmitters with a slight difference in the wording"
4591,"It has been narrated on the authority of Abu Huraira who said:We were (sitting) in the mosque when the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to us and said: (Let us) go to the Jews. We went out with him until we came to them. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) stood up and called out to them (saying): O ye assembly of Jews"
4592,"It has been narrated on the authority of Ibn Umar that the Jews of Banu Nadir and Banu Quraiza fought against the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) who expelled Banu Nadir"
4593,"A similar hadith has been transmitted by a different chain of narrators"
4594,"It has been narrated by 'Umar b. al-Khattib that he heard the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) say:I will expel the Jews and Christians from the Arabian Peninsula and will not leave any but Muslim"
4595,"This hadith has been narrated on the authority of Zubair with the same chain of transmitters"
4596,"It has been narrated on the authority of Abu Sa'id al-Khudri who said:The people of Quraiza surrendered accepting the decision of Sa'd b. Mu'adh about them. Accordingly"
4597,"Through the same chain of transmitters Shu'ba has narrated the same tradition in which he says that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said (to Sa'd):You have adjudged according to the command of God. And once he said: you have adjudged by the decision of a king"
4598,"It has been narrated on the authority of A'isha who said:Sa'd was wounded on the day of the Battle of the Ditch. A man from the Quraish called Ibn al-Ariqah shot at him an arrow which pierced the artery in the middle of his forearm. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) pitched a tent for him in the mosque and would inquire after him being in close proximity. When he returned from the Ditch and laid down his arms and took a bath"
4599,"It has been narrated on the authority of Hisham (who learnt it from his father) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said (to Sa'd):You have adjudged their case with the judgment of God. the Exalted and Glorified"
4600,"It has been narrated on the authority of 'A'isha that Sa'd's wound became dry and was going to heal when he prayed:O God"
4601,"This tradition has been narrated by Hishim through the same chain of transmitters with a little difference in the wording. He said:(His wound) began to bleed that very night and it continued to bleed until he died. He has made the addition that it was then that (a non-believing) poet said: Hark"
4602,"It has been narrated on the authority of Abdullah who said:On the day he returned from the Battle of Ahzab"
4603,"It has been narrated on the authority of Anas b. Malik who said:When the Muhajirs migrated from Mecca to Medina; they came (in a state that) they had not anything (i. e. money) in theirhands"
4604,"It has been narrated by Anas that (after his migration to Medina) a person placed at the Prophet's (ﷺ) disposal some date-palms growing on his land until the lands of Quraiza and Nadir were conquered. Then he began to return to him whatever he had received. (In this connection) my people told me to approach the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and ask from him what his people had given him or a portion thereof"
4605,"It has been narrated on the authority of Abdullah b. Mughaffal who said I found a bag containing fat on the day of the Battle of Khaibar. I caught hold of it and said:I will not give anything today from it to anybody. Then I turned round and saw that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was smiling (at my words)"
4606,"This tradition has been transmitted by a different chain of narrators with a different wording"
4607,"Ishaq bin Ibrahim al-Hanzali, Ibn Abi 'Umar, Muhammad bin Rafi', and 'Abd bin Humaid narrated to us - and the wording is that of Ibn Rafi' - Ibn Rafi' and Ibn Abi 'Umar said: ""Abd ar-Razzaq narrated to us,"" while the other two said: ""Abd ar-Razzaq informed us"": Ma'mar informed us, from az-Zuhri, from 'Ubaidullah bin 'Abdullah bin 'Utbah, from Ibn 'Abbas: that Abu Sufyan told him directly, mouth to mouth, saying: I set out during the truce that was between me and the Messenger of Allah (may peace be upon him). He said: While I was in Syria, a letter was brought from the Messenger of Allah (may peace be upon him) to Heraclius, that is, the great one of the Byzantines. He said: Dihyah al-Kalbi had brought it and delivered it to the governor of Busra, who delivered it to Heraclius. Heraclius said: ""Is there anyone here from the people of this man who claims to be a prophet?"" They said: ""Yes."" He said: So I was called along with a group of Quraysh, and we entered upon Heraclius, and he seated us before him. He said: ""Which of you is closest in lineage to this man who claims to be a prophet?"" Abu Sufyan said: I said, ""I am,"" so they seated me before him and seated my companions behind me. Then he called for his interpreter and said to him: ""Tell them that I am going to question this man about the one who claims to be a prophet, and if he lies to me, contradict him.""

Abu Sufyan said: By Allah, were it not for the fear that lying would be attributed to me, I would have lied. Then he said to his interpreter: ""Ask him how is his lineage among you?"" I said: ""He is of noble lineage among us."" He said: ""Was any of his forefathers a king?"" I said: ""No."" He said: ""Did you used to accuse him of lying before he said what he said?"" I said: ""No."" He said: ""Do the noble people follow him, or the weak ones?"" I said: ""Rather the weak ones."" He said: ""Are they increasing or decreasing?"" I said: ""No, rather they are increasing."" He said: ""Does anyone among them turn back from his religion after having entered it, out of dislike for it?"" I said: ""No."" He said: ""Have you fought him?"" I said: ""Yes."" He said: ""How was your fighting with him?"" I said: ""The war between us and him goes back and forth; he inflicts losses on us and we inflict losses on him."" He said: ""Does he betray trust?"" I said: ""No, and we are currently in a truce with him, and we do not know what he will do during it."" He (Abu Sufyan) said: By Allah, I found no opportunity to insert anything into my speech except this one word.

He said: ""Has anyone said this saying before him?"" I said: ""No."" He said to his interpreter: ""Tell him: I asked you about his lineage, and you claimed that he is of noble lineage among you - and thus messengers are sent from among the noble lineages of their people. I asked you whether any of his forefathers was a king, and you claimed not; so I said, had one of his forefathers been a king, I would have said he is a man seeking the kingdom of his forefathers. I asked you about his followers, whether they are the weak or the noble, and you said the weak - and they are the followers of the messengers. I asked you whether you used to accuse him of lying before he said what he said, and you claimed not; so I knew that he would not have abstained from lying about people only to go and lie about Allah. I asked you whether anyone turns back from his religion after entering it, out of dislike for it, and you claimed not - and such is faith when it mixes with the joy of hearts. I asked you whether they are increasing or decreasing, and you claimed they are increasing - and such is faith until it is complete. I asked you whether you fought him, and you claimed that you did fight him, and that the war between you goes back and forth, he inflicting losses on you and you inflicting losses on him - and thus the messengers are tested, then the final outcome is theirs. I asked you whether he betrays trust, and you claimed that he does not betray trust - and thus the messengers do not betray trust. I asked you whether anyone said this saying before him, and you claimed not; so I said, had anyone said this saying before him, I would have said he is a man following a saying that was said before him.""

He said: Then he said: ""What does he command you to do?"" I said: ""He commands us to pray, to give zakat, to maintain ties of kinship, and to be chaste."" He said: ""If what you say about him is true, then he is indeed a prophet. I already knew that he was going to appear, but I did not think he would be from among you. If I knew that I could reach him, I would love to meet him, and if I were with him, I would wash his feet, and his dominion will indeed reach what is beneath my feet.""

He said: Then he called for the letter of the Messenger of Allah (may peace be upon him) and read it, and it was:

""In the name of Allah, the Most Gracious, the Most Merciful. From Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah, to Heraclius, the great one of the Byzantines. Peace be upon whoever follows the guidance. Now then, I invite you with the call of Islam. Submit and you will be safe, submit and Allah will give you your reward twice over; but if you turn away, then upon you is the sin of the peasantry (the common people). And O People of the Book, come to a common word between us and you: that we worship none but Allah, and that we associate no partners with Him, and that none of us take others as lords besides Allah. But if they turn away, then say: Bear witness that we are Muslims (submitters to Allah).""

He said: When he finished reading the letter, voices were raised around him and there was much clamor, and he ordered us to be taken out, so we were taken out. He said: I said to my companions when we went out: ""The matter of Ibn Abi Kabshah (i.e., Muhammad) has become significant - indeed the king of the Byzantines fears him!"" He said: And I continued to be certain of the matter of the Messenger of Allah (may peace be upon him), that he would prevail, until Allah caused Islam to enter into me."
4608,"This hadith has been narrated on the authority of Ibn Shihab with the same chain of transmitters but with the addition:"""" When Allah inflicted defeat on the armies of Persia"
4609,"It has been narrated on the authority of Anas that the Prophet of Allah (ﷺ) wrote to Chosroes (King of Persia)"
4610,"The tradition has been narrated on the authority of Anas b. Malik (the same narrator) through a different chain of transmitters"
4611,"It has been narrated on the authority of the same narrator through another chain of transmitters with the same difference in the wording"
4612,"It has been narrated on the authority of 'Abbas who said:I was in the company of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on the Day of Hunain. I and Abd Sufyan b. Harith b. 'Abd al-Muttalib stuck to the Messenaer of Allah (ﷺ) and we did not separate from him. And the Messenger of Allah (may place be upon him) was riding on his white mule which had been presented to him by Farwa b. Nufitha al-Judhami. When the Muslims had an encounter with the disbelievers"
4613,"A version of the tradition has been transmitted through another chain of narrators. In this version the words uttered by the Prophet (ﷺ) (after he had thrown the pebbles in the face of the enemy) are reported as:"""" By the Lord of the Ka'ba"
4614,"Abbas reported:I was with Allah's Apostle (ﷺ) on the Day of Hunain. The rest of the hadith is the same but with this variation that the hadith transmitted by Yonus and Ma'mar is more detailed and complete"
4615,"It has been narratedon the authority of Abu Ishaq who said:A man asked Bara' (b. 'Azib): Did you run away on the Day of Hunain. O"
4616,"It has been narrated (through a different chain of transmitters) by Abu Ishiq that a person said to Bara' (b. 'Azib):Abu Umara"
4617,"It has been narrated through a still different chain of transmitters by the same narrator (i. e. Abu Ishaq) who said:I heard from Bara' who was asked by a man from the Qais tribe: Did you run away from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on the Day of Hunain? Bara' said: But the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) did not run away. On that day Banu Hawzzin took part in the battle as archers (on the side of the disbelievers). When we attacked them"
4618,"This hadith has been narrated on the authority of Bara' with another chain of transmitters"
4619,"This tradition has been narrated on the authority of Salama who said:We fought by the side of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) at Hunain. When we encountered the enemy"
4620,"It has been narrated on the authority of Ibn 'Amr who said:The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) besieged the people of Ta'if"
4621,"It has been narrated on the authority of Anas that when (the news of) the advance of Abu Sufyan (at the head of a force) reached him. the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) held consultations with his Companions. The narrator said:Abu Bakr spoke (expressing his own views)"
4622,"Shayban bin Farrukh narrated to us, Sulayman bin al-Mughirah narrated to us, Thabit al-Bunani narrated to us, from Abdullah bin Rabah, from Abu Hurairah, who said: ""Delegations came to Mu'awiyah, and that was during Ramadan. Some of us used to prepare food for one another, and Abu Hurairah was among those who frequently invited us to his lodging. So I said: 'Should I not prepare food and invite them to my lodging?' So I ordered that food be prepared, then I met Abu Hurairah in the evening and said: 'The invitation is at my place tonight.' He said: I said: 'Yes.' So I invited them, and Abu Hurairah said: 'Shall I not inform you of a hadith from your own accounts, O company of the Ansar?' Then he mentioned the conquest of Makkah, and said:

The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) advanced until he arrived at Makkah. He sent al-Zubair in command of one flank, and sent Khalid in command of the other flank, and sent Abu Ubaidah in charge of those on foot without armor. They took the bottom of the valley, while the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was with the main army column. He said: The Prophet (ﷺ) looked and saw me. Abu Hurairah said: I said: 'Here I am, O Messenger of Allah.' He said: 'None should come to me except an Ansari.'""

Others besides Shayban added: He said: ""Call out to me the Ansar."" He said: So they surrounded him, while the Quraysh had gathered a rabble of their lowly followers and dependents, and they said: ""We will put these forward; if they achieve something we will be with them, and if they are struck down, we will give what is asked of us."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Do you see the rabble of Quraysh and their followers?"" Then he said, with one of his hands over the other: ""[Advance] until you meet me at al-Safa."" He said: So we set off, and none of us wished to kill anyone but that he killed him, and none of them turned anything against us. He said: Then Abu Sufyan came and said: ""O Messenger of Allah, the green pastures of Quraysh have been laid open; there is no Quraysh after today."" Then he [the Prophet] said: ""Whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe."" The Ansar said among themselves: ""As for the man, he has been overtaken by a desire for his hometown and tenderness towards his kinsfolk."" Abu Hurairah said: Then the revelation came - and when the revelation came, it was not hidden from us; when it came, no one would raise his eyes to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) until the revelation was concluded. When the revelation ended, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""O company of the Ansar!"" They said: ""Here we are, O Messenger of Allah."" He said: ""Did you say: 'As for the man, he has been overtaken by a desire for his hometown'?"" They said: ""That was indeed said."" He said: ""No indeed! I am the servant of Allah and His Messenger. I emigrated to Allah and to you; my life is your life and my death is your death."" So they came forward to him weeping, saying: ""By Allah, we said what we said only out of protectiveness for Allah and His Messenger."" The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: ""Indeed Allah and His Messenger believe you and accept your excuse.""

He said: Then the people went to the house of Abu Sufyan, and the people closed their doors. He said: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) advanced until he came to the Stone and touched it, then he circled the House. He said: Then he came upon an idol beside the House which they used to worship. He said: In the hand of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was a bow, and he was holding it by its curved tip. When he came to the idol, he began to jab it in its eye, saying: ""The truth has come, and falsehood has vanished."" When he finished his circumambulation, he went to al-Safa and climbed upon it until he could see the House, and he raised his hands, and began to praise Allah and to pray with whatever he wished to pray for."
4623,"The tradition has been narrated by a different chain of transmitters with the following additions:(i) Then be (the Messenger of Allah) said with his hands one upon the other: Kill them (who stand in your way).... (ii) They (the Ansar) replied: We said so"
4624,"It has been narrated on the authority of Abdullah b. Rabah who said:We came to Mu'awiya b. Abu Sufyan as a deputation and Abu Huraira was among us. Each of us would prepare food for his companions turn by turn for a day. (Accordingly) when it was my turn I said: Abu Huraira"
4625,"It has been narrated by Ibn Abdullah who said:The Prophet (ﷺ) entered Mecca. There were three hundred and sixty idols around the Ka'ba. He began to thrust them with the stick that was in his hand saying:"""" Truth has come and falsehood has vanished. Lo! falsehood was destined to vanish"""" (xvii. 8). Truth has arrived"
4626,"This tradition has been narrated by Ibn Abu Najah through a different chain of transmitters up to the word:Zahaqa"
4627,"It has been narrated on the authority of Abdullah b. Muti' who heard from his father and said:I heard the Prophet (ﷺ) say on the day of the Conquest of Mecca: No Quraishite will be killed hound hand and foot from this day until the Day of judgment"
4628,"The same tradition has been narrated on the authority of Zakriyya through the same chain of transmitters with the following addition:"""" No rebellious Quraishite with al-Asi as his name embraced Islam that day except Muti. His name-was al-Asi"
4629,"It has been narrated on the authority of al-Bara' b. 'Azib who said:'Ali b. Abu Talib penned the treaty between the Prophet (ﷺ) and the polytheists on the Day of Hudaibiya. He wrote: This is what Muhammad"
4630,"It has been narrated on the authority of Abu Ishaq"
4631,"It has been narrated on the authority of Bara' who said:When the Prophet (ﷺ) was checked from going to the Ka'ba"
4632,"It has been narrated on the authority of Anas that the Quraish made peace with the Prophet (ﷺ). Among them was Suhail b. Amr. The Prophet (ﷺ) said to 'Ali:Write"""" In the name of Allah"
4633,"It has been narrated on the authority of Abu Wa'il who said:Sahal b. Hunaif stood up on the Day of Siffin and said: O ye people"
4634,"It has been narrated on the authority of Shaqiq who said:I heard Sahl b. Hunaif say at Siffin: O ye people"
4635,"The same tradition has been narrated through a different chain of transmitters on the authority of A'mash. This version contains the words:Ila amrin yofzi'una instead of Ila amrin na'rifuhu"
4636,"It has been narrated through a different chain of transmitters on the authority of Abu Wa'il who said:I heard Sahl b. Hunaif say at Siffin: Blame (the hollowness) of your views about your religion. I thought to myself on the day of Abu Jandal that if I could turn down the order of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)"
4637,"It has been narrated on the authority of Anas b. Malik who said:When they (Companions of the Holy Prophet) were overwhelmed with grief and distress on his return from Hudaibiya where he had slaughtered his sacrificial beasts (not being allowed to proceed to Mecca)"
4638,"This tradition has been narrated through a different chain of transmitters"
4639,"It has been reported on the authority of Hudbaifa b. al-Yaman who said:Nothing prevented me from being present at! he Battle of Badr except this incident. I came out with my father Husail (to participate in the Battle)"
4640,"It has been narrated by Ibrahim al-Taimi on the authority of his father who said:We were sitting in the company of Hudhaifa. A man said: If I were in the time of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)"
4641,"It has been reported on the authority of Anas b. Malik that (when the enemy got the upper hand) on the day of the Battle of Uhud"
4642,"It has been narrated on the authority of Abd-ul-'Aziz b. Abu Hazim"
4643,"It has been reported on the authority of Abu Hazim who heard from Sahl b. Sa'd. The latter was asked about the injury of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). He said:By God"
4644,"The same tradition has been narrated on the authority of Sahl b. Sa'd through a different chain of transmitters with a slight difference in the wording"
4645,"It has been narrated on the authority of Anas that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had his front teeth damaged on the day of the Battle of Uhud"
4646,"It has been narrated on the authority of 'Abdullah who said:It appeared to me as if I saw the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) (and heard him) relate the story of a Prophet who had been beaten by his people"
4647,"A version of the tradition with a slightly different wording has been narrated by another chain of transmitters"
4648,"It has been narrated by Hammam b. Munabbih who said:This is what has been related to us by Abu Huraira from the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). (With this introduction) he narrated a number of traditions. One of these was that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: Great is the wrath of Allah upon a people who have done this to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ)"
4649,"It has been narrated on the authority of Ibn Mas'ud who said:While the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was saying his prayer near the Ka'ba and Abu Jahl with his companions was sitting (near by)"
4650,"It has been narrated by Abdullah (b. Mas'ud) who said:When the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was lying postrate in prayer and around him were some people from the Quraish"
4651,"Abu Ishiq has narrated a similar tradition through a different chain of transmitters and has added:He (the Messenger of Allah) loved to repeat the supplication thrice. He was saying: O Allah"
4652,"It has been narrated on the authority of 'Abdullah that"
4653,"It has been narrated on the authority of `A'isha, the wife of the Prophet ( صلی اللہ علیہ وسلم ), who said to the Messenger of Allah (may peace he upon him):
Messenger of Allah, has there come upon you a day more terrible than the day of Uhud. He said: I have experienced from thy people and the hardest treatment I met from them was what I received from them on the day of `Aqaba. I betook myself to Ibn `Abd Yalil b. `Abd Kulal with the purpose of inviting him to Islam, but he did not respond to me as I desired. So I departed with signs of (deep) distress on my face. I did not recover until I reached Qarn al-Tha`alib. Where I raised my head, lo! near me was a cloud which had cast its shadow on me. I looked and lo! there was in it the angel Jibril who called out to me and said: God, the Honoured and Glorious, has heard what thy people have said to thee, and how they have reacted to thy call. And He has sent to thee the angel in charge of the mountains so that thou mayest order him what thou wishest (him to do) with regard to them. The angel in charge of the mountains (then) called out to me, greeted me and said: Muhammad, God has listened to what thy people have said to thee. I am the angel in charge of the mountains, and thy Lord has sent me to thee so that thou mayest order me what thou wishest. If thou wishest that I should bring together the two mountains that stand opposite to each other at the extremities of Mecca to crush them in between, (I would do that). But the Messenger of Allah (may peace he upon him) said to him: I rather hope that God will produce from their descendants such persons as will worship Allah, the One, and will not ascribe partners to Him."
4654,"It has been narrated on the authority of Jundub b. Sufyan who said:A finger of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was wounded in one of the encounters He said: Thou art just a little finger which has bled"
4655,"It has been narrated on the authority of Aswad b. Qais who said:The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was in a cave (or raid) when his finger was hurt"
4656,"It has been narrated on the authority of Aswad b. Qais who heard Jundub saying that Gabriel delayed his visit to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) The polytheists began to say that Muhammad has been forsaken. At this Allah"
4657,"It has been narrated on the authority of Aswad b. Qais who said:I heard Jundub b. Sufyan say: The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) fell ill and did not wake up for two or three nights (for prayers) A woman came to him and said: Muhammad"
4658,"This hadith has been narrated on the authority of Aswad b. Qais with the same chain of transmitters"
4659,"It has been narrated on the authority of Usama b. Zaid that the Prophet (ﷺ) rode a donkey. It had on it a saddle under which was a mattress made at Fadak (a place near Medina). Behind him he seated Usama. He was going to the street of Banu Harith al-Khazraj to inquire after the health of Sa'd b. Ubada This happened before the Battle of Badr. (He proceeded) until he passed by a mixed company of people in which were Muslims"
4660,"A similar tradition has been narrated through a different chain of transmitters by Ibn Shihab with the addition of the words:"""" Before Abdullah (b. Ubayy) became a Muslim"
4661,"It has been narrated on the authority of Anas b. Malik that it was said to the Prophet (ﷺ):Would that you approached Abdullah b. Ubayy (to persuade him to accept Islam). The Prophet (ﷺ) (accordingly) went to him"
4662,"It has been narrated on the authority of Anas b. Malik that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said (after the encounter at Badr):Who will ascertain for us what has happened to Abu Jahl? Ibn Mas'ud went (to gather this information). He found that the two sons of 'Afra' had struck him and he lay cold at the point of death. He caught him by his beard and said: Art thou Abu Jahl? He said: is there anybody superior to the person you have killed"
4663,"A similar tradition has been transmitted by a different chain of narrators"
4664,"It has been narrated on the authority of Jabir that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:Who will kill Ka'b b. Ashraf? He has maligned Allah"
4665,"It has been narrated on the authority of Anas that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) raided Khaibar. One morning we offered prayers in the darkness of early dawn (near Khaibar). Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) mounted (his horse). Abu Talha mounted his and I mounted behind Abu Talha on the same horse. The Prophet of Allah (ﷺ) rode through the streets of Khaibar and (I rode so close to him) that my knee touched the thigh of the Prophet of Allah (ﷺ). The wrapper got aside from his thigh"
4666,"It has been narrated (through another chain of transmitters) on the authority of the same narrator (i. e. Anas) who said:I was riding behind Abu Talha on the day of the Battle of Khaibar (and we were riding so close to the Prophet that) my foot would touch his We encountered the people at sunrise when they had come out with their axes"
4667,"This hadith has been transmitted on the authority of Anas b. Malik with a slight variation of words"
4668,"It has been narrated on the authority of Salama b. al-Akwa' who said:We marched upon Khaibar with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). We journeyed during the night. One of the people said to (my brother) 'Amir b. al-Akwa': Won't you recite to us some of your verses? Amir was a poet. So he began to chant his verses to urge the camels"
4669,"It has been reported on the authority of Salama b. Akwa' who said:On the day of the Battle of Khaibar my brother fought a fierce fight by the side of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). His sword rebounded and killed him. The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (may peace be upon hill) talked about his death and doubted (whether it was martyrdom). (They said): (He is) a man killed by his own weapon"
4670,"It has been reported on the authority of Barra' who said:The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) was carrying the earth with us on the Day of Ahzab and the whiteness of his belly had been covered with earth. (While engaged in this toil) he was reciting: By God"
4671,"It has been narrated on the authority of Abu Ishaq who said:I heard from Bara' a similar tradition except that he said:"""" These people (the Meccans) rebelled against us"
4672,"It has been reported on the authority of Sahl b. Sa'd who said:The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) came to us while we were digging the ditch and were carrying the earth on our shoulders. (Seeing our condition)"
4673,"It has been narrated on the authority of Anas b. Malik that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said:O God"
4674,"According to another version of the tradition, reported on the authority of Anas b. Malik, the Messenger of Allah (may peace he upon him) is reported to have said:
O God, there is no life but the life of the Hereafter, So grant honour to the Ansar and the Muhajirs."
4675,"According to still another version of the tradition narrated by the same authority"
4676,"It has been narrated (through a still difterent chain of transmitters) by Anas that the Companions of Muhammad (ﷺ) were chanting on the day of the Battle of the Ditch:We are those who have sworn allegiance to Muhammad (And made a covenant with him) to follow Islam as long as we live. Hammad is not sure whether Anas said:"""" Ala'l-Islam"""" or"
4677,"It has been narrated on the authority of Yazid b. Abu 'Ubaid who said that he heard Salama b. al-Akwa' say:I went out before the Adhan for the morning prayer had been delivered. The milch she-camels of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) were grazing at Dhu Qarad. 'Abd al-Rahman b. Auf's slave met me and said: The milch she-camels of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had been taken away. I said: Who has taken them away? He said: (the people belonging to the tribe of) Ghatafan. I cried thrice: Help! I made the whole city between the two lavas hear my cry. Then I ran straight in their pursuit until I overtook them at Dhu Qarad where they were just going to water their animals. I"
4678,"Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah told us: Hashim ibn al-Qasim told us. (Another chain:) And Ishaq ibn Ibrahim told us: Abu 'Amir al-'Aqadi informed us - both of them from 'Ikrimah ibn 'Ammar. (Another chain:) And 'Abdullah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman al-Darimi told us - and this is his wording - Abu 'Ali al-Hanafi 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abd al-Majid informed us: 'Ikrimah, that is, ibn 'Ammar, told us: Iyas ibn Salamah told me: My father told me, he said:

""We came to al-Hudaybiyah with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and we were fourteen hundred men, with fifty sheep that could not satisfy [our thirst if we had to rely on water for them alone – i.e. water was scarce]. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sat at the edge of the well, and either he prayed or he spat into it, and it gushed forth, so we drank and drew water for ourselves.

Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) called us for the pledge of allegiance beneath the tree. I gave him my pledge first among the people, then others pledged one after another, until when he was in the middle of the people he said: 'Give the pledge, O Salamah.' I said: 'I have already pledged to you, O Messenger of Allah, at the very first of the people.' He said: 'Do so again.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) saw me without any weapon, so he gave me a small round shield or a leather shield. Then he continued taking pledges until, when he was at the end of the people, he said: 'Will you not pledge to me, O Salamah?' I said: 'I have already pledged to you, O Messenger of Allah, at the beginning of the people and in the middle of the people.' He said: 'Do so again,' so I pledged to him a third time.

Then he said to me: 'O Salamah, where is your shield that I gave you?' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, my uncle 'Amir met me without a weapon, so I gave it to him.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) laughed and said: 'You are like the one who said: O Allah, grant me a beloved companion who is dearer to me than myself.'

Then the polytheists sent messages to us seeking peace, until some of us mixed with others and we made peace. I was a follower of Talhah ibn 'Ubaydullah - I watered his horse, groomed it, served him, and ate from his food - having left my family and wealth, emigrating to Allah and His Messenger (ﷺ).

When we and the people of Makkah made peace and mixed with one another, I went to a tree and cleared away its thorns, then lay down at its base. Four polytheists from the people of Makkah came to me and began to speak ill of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), so I disliked them and moved to another tree. They hung up their weapons and lay down. While they were like that, a caller called out from the bottom of the valley: 'O Muhajirun, Ibn Zunaym has been killed!' So I drew my sword and rushed upon those four while they were sleeping, and I took their weapons and gathered them in a bundle in my hand. Then I said: 'By the One who honored the face of Muhammad, none of you shall raise his head but I will strike the place where his eyes are.' Then I brought them, driving them before me, to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).

My uncle 'Amir also came with a man from the 'Abalat called Mikraz, leading him to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) on a horse, armored, along with seventy of the polytheists. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) looked at them and said: 'Leave them - let the beginning of the wrongdoing and its repetition be upon them,' and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) pardoned them. Then Allah revealed: 'And it is He who withheld their hands from you and your hands from them within Makkah after He had given you victory over them' (Surah al-Fath, 48:24) - the whole verse.

Then we set out returning to Madinah, and we camped at a place where there was a mountain between us and Banu Lihyan, who were polytheists. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sought forgiveness for whoever would climb this mountain that night, as if he would be a lookout for the Prophet (ﷺ) and his companions. Salamah said: I climbed it that night twice or three times.

Then we came to Madinah, and the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) sent the pack animals with Rabah, the freed slave of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), and I was with him. I went out with him with Talhah's horse, to graze it along with the pack animals. When morning came, 'Abd al-Rahman al-Fazari had raided the pack animals of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), driving them all away and killing the shepherd.

I said: 'O Rabah, take this horse and deliver it to Talhah ibn 'Ubaydullah, and inform the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) that the polytheists have raided his grazing animals.' Then I stood on a hillock, facing Madinah, and called out three times: 'O people, to arms!' Then I went out following the tracks of the group, shooting arrows at them and reciting verses, saying: 'I am the son of al-Akwa', and today is the day of the lowly ones!' I caught up with a man of them and struck him with an arrow in his saddle-pack until the arrowhead reached his shoulder. I said: 'Take this - I am the son of al-Akwa', and today is the day of the lowly ones!'

By Allah, I continued shooting at them and hamstringing their horses. Whenever a horseman turned back toward me, I would go to a tree and sit at its base, then shoot at him and hamstring his mount, until when the mountain pass narrowed and they entered its narrow part, I climbed the mountain and began throwing stones down on them. I continued like that, following them, until Allah left none of the camels of the Messenger of Allah's (ﷺ) pack animals except that I drove it back behind me, and they left it between me and them. Then I continued following them, shooting at them, until they threw down more than thirty cloaks and thirty spears, trying to lighten their load. They did not throw down anything without my placing markers of stones over it, which the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and his companions would recognize.

Until they came to a narrow mountain pass, where a certain man, Ibn Badr al-Fazari, met them, and they sat down to have their midday meal. I sat on the top of a hill. Al-Fazari said: 'What is this that I see?' They said: 'We have suffered hardship from this man - by Allah, he has not left us since dawn, shooting at us until he has stripped everything from our hands.' He said: 'Let four men of you go up to him.' So four of them climbed up the mountain toward me. When they were within speaking range, I said: 'Do you know me?' They said: 'No, who are you?' I said: 'I am Salamah ibn al-Akwa'. By the One who honored the face of Muhammad (ﷺ), I will not pursue any man of you but I will catch him, and no man of you will pursue me and catch me.' One of them said: 'I think that's true.' So they went back.

I did not move from my place until I saw the horsemen of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) threading through the trees. The first of them was al-Akhram al-Asadi, followed by Abu Qatadah al-Ansari, followed by al-Miqdad ibn al-Aswad al-Kindi. I took hold of al-Akhram's reins as they [the enemy] turned to flee. I said: 'O Akhram, beware of them, lest they cut you off before the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) and his companions catch up.' He said: 'O Salamah, if you believe in Allah and the Last Day, and know that Paradise is true and the Fire is true, do not come between me and martyrdom.' So I let him go, and he met 'Abd al-Rahman. He hamstrung 'Abd al-Rahman's horse, and 'Abd al-Rahman stabbed him and killed him, then turned onto his horse. Abu Qatadah, the horseman of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), caught up with 'Abd al-Rahman and stabbed him, killing him.

By the One who honored the face of Muhammad (ﷺ), I followed them running on my own two feet, until I could no longer see any of the companions of Muhammad (ﷺ) behind me, nor any dust of theirs, until they turned before sunset toward a mountain pass with water in it called Dhu Qarad, to drink from it, being thirsty. They saw me running behind them, so I drove them away from it, and they did not taste a drop of it. They went out and hurried onward through a mountain pass. I ran and caught up with a man of them and struck him with an arrow in the top of his shoulder. I said: 'Take this - I am the son of al-Akwa', and today is the day of the lowly ones!' He said: 'May his mother be bereaved of him - is this Akwa' at dawn?' I said: 'Yes, O enemy of yourself, this is your Akwa' at dawn.'

They abandoned two horses at a mountain pass, and I brought them, driving them, to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). 'Amir caught up with me with a water-skin containing some diluted milk and another water-skin containing water, so I performed ablution and drank. Then I came to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who was at the water from which I had driven them away. The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had taken those camels and everything I had recovered from the polytheists, every spear and cloak. Bilal had slaughtered a camel from among those I had recovered from the group, and he was roasting some of its liver and hump for the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).

I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, let me go and I will select a hundred men from the people and pursue the group, so that not one of them who could report [what happened] will remain but I will kill him.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) laughed until his molars showed in the light of the fire, and said: 'O Salamah, do you think you could do that?' I said: 'Yes, by the One who has honored you.' He said: 'They are now being given hospitality in the land of Ghatafan.'

Then a man from Ghatafan came and said: 'A certain man slaughtered a camel for them, and when they skinned it, they saw dust [of approaching riders], and said: ""The enemy has come upon you!"" so they fled.'

When morning came, the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'The best of our horsemen today was Abu Qatadah, and the best of our footmen was Salamah.' Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) gave me two shares - the share of a horseman and the share of a footman - combining them both for me. Then the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) had me ride behind him on al-'Adba', returning to Madinah.

While we were traveling, there was a man from the Ansar who could not be outrun in a race. He kept saying: 'Is there anyone to race to Madinah? Is there a racer?' He kept repeating this. When I heard what he said, I said: 'Will you not honor someone honorable, nor fear someone noble?' He said: 'No, unless it is the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ).' I said: 'O Messenger of Allah, by my father and mother, let me race the man.' He said: 'If you wish.' I said [to the man]: 'Go on then,' and I bent my legs and leapt up and ran. I paced myself for a stretch or two, conserving my breath, then ran after him, pacing myself for another stretch or two. Then I put on speed until I caught up with him, and I struck him between his shoulders. I said: 'You have been outrun, by Allah!' He said: 'I think so.' So I outran him to Madinah.

By Allah, we had not stayed but three nights when we set out to Khaybar with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). My uncle 'Amir began reciting verses of poetry to the people: 'By Allah, were it not for Allah, we would not have been guided, nor given charity, nor prayed. And we cannot do without Your favor, so make our feet firm if we meet [the enemy], and send down tranquility upon us.' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Who is this?' He said: 'I am 'Amir.' He said: 'May your Lord forgive you.' And the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) never sought forgiveness specifically for a person except that he was martyred. 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, who was on his camel, called out: 'O Prophet of Allah, if only you had let us enjoy 'Amir [longer]!'

When we arrived at Khaybar, their king Marhab came out brandishing his sword and saying: 'Khaybar knows well that I am Marhab, well-armed, a tried and tested hero, when wars come blazing forth.' My uncle 'Amir came forward to face him and said: 'Khaybar knows well that I am 'Amir, well-armed, a daring hero.' They exchanged two strikes, and Marhab's sword struck 'Amir's shield, and 'Amir went to strike low at him, but his sword rebounded upon himself and cut his own artery, and that was the cause of his death.

Salamah said: I went out, and there were some men from the companions of the Prophet (ﷺ) saying: ''Amir's deed is nullified - he killed himself.' I came to the Prophet (ﷺ) weeping, and said: 'O Messenger of Allah, 'Amir's deed is nullified!' The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) said: 'Whoever said that has lied. Rather, he has his reward twice over.'

Then he sent me to 'Ali, who had an eye ailment, saying: 'I will surely give the banner to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger, or whom Allah and His Messenger love.' I went and brought 'Ali, leading him by the hand while his eyes were sore, until I brought him to the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ), who spat in his eyes, and he was cured. He gave him the banner, and Marhab came out and said: 'Khaybar knows well that I am Marhab, well-armed, a tried and tested hero, when wars come blazing forth.' 'Ali said: 'I am the one whom my mother named Haydarah, like a lion of the forests, fearsome in appearance. I will give them full measure with the measure of the sandarah.' Then he struck Marhab's head and killed him, and the conquest was achieved through his hands.""

Ibrahim said: Muhammad ibn Yahya told us: 'Abd al-Samad ibn 'Abd al-Warith told us, from 'Ikrimah ibn 'Ammar, this hadith in its full length. And Ahmad ibn Yusuf al-Azdi al-Sulami told us: al-Nadr ibn Muhammad told us, from 'Ikrimah ibn 'Ammar, similarly."
4679,"It has been narrated on the authority of Anas b. Malik that eighty Persons from the inhabitants of Mecca swooped down upon the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) from the mountain of Tan'im. They were armed and wanted to attack the Prophet (ﷺ) and his Companions unawares. He (the Holy Prophet) captured them but spared their lives. So"
4680,"It has been narrated on the authority of Anas that"
4681,"It has been narrated on the authority of Anas that"
4682,"It has been narrated on the authority of Anas b. Malik who said that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) allowed Umm Sulaim and some other women of the Ansar to accompany him when he went to war; they would give water (to the soldiers) and would treat the wounded"
4683,"It has been narrated on the authority of Anas b. Malik who said:On the Day of Uhud some of the people"
4684,"It has been narrated on the authority of Yazid b. Hurmuz that Najda wrote to Ibn Abbas inquiring of him five things. Ibn Abbas said:If I had not the fear of committing (sin) for concealing the knowledge I would not have written to him. Najda wrote to him saying (after praising the Almighty and invoking blessings on the Prophet): Tell me whether the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) took women to participate with him in Jihad; (if he did)"
4685,"This tradition has been narrated by the game authority (Yazid b. Hurmus) through a different chain of transmitters with the following difference in the elucidation of one of the points raised by Najda in his letter to Ibn Abas:The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used not to kill the children"
4686,"It has been narrated on the authority of Yazid b. Hurmuz who said:Najda b. 'Amir al-Haruri wrote to Ibn Abbas asking him about the slave and the woman as to whether they would get a share from the booty (it they participated in Jihad) ; about the killing of (enemy) children (in war) ; about the orphan as to when his orphanhood comes to an end; about kinsmen (of the Holy Prophet) as to who they are. He said to Yazid: Write to him. (If he were not likely to fall into folly"
4687,"This hadith has been narrated on the authority of Yazid b. Hurmuz through another chain of transmitters"
4688,"It has been narrated on the anthority of Yazid b. Hurmuz who said:Najda wrote to Ibn Abbas. I was sitting in the company of Ibn 'Abbas when he read his letter and wrote its reply. Ibn Abbas said: Were it not for preventing him from falling into wickedness. I would not have replied to his letter"
4689,"This hadith has been transmitted on the authority of Yazid b. Hurmuz. but not complete (as we find in the above mentioned ahadith)"
4690,"It has been narrated on the authority of Umm 'Atiyya"
4691,"A similar tradition has been narrated on the authority of Hisham b. Hassan through a different chain of transmitters"
4692,"It has been narrated on the authority of Abu Ishaq that 'Abdullah b. Yazid went (out of the city) with people for offering"""" Istisqa"""" ' prayer (for rainfall). He offered two rak'ahs. Then he prayed for rain. That day I met Zaid b. Arqam. There was only one man between me and him (at that time). I asked him:How many military expeditions did the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) undertake? He said: Nineteen expeditions. I asked him: On how many expeditions did you accompany him? He said: On seventeen expeditions. I asked: Which was the first expedition he led? He answered: Dhat-ul-"
4693,"It has been narrated on the authority of Zaid b. Arqam that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) fought nineteen battles and after the Migration performed only one Pilgrimage called Hajjat-ul-Wada"
4694,"It has been reported on the authority of Abu Zubair who heard Jabir b. `Abdullah say:I fought in the company of the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) nineteen battles. Jabir said: I did not participate in the Battle of Badr and the Battle of Uhud. My father prevented me (from participating in these battles as my age was tender). After `Abdullah (my father) was killed on the Day of Uhud"
4695,"It has been narrated on the authority of Buraida (who heard the tradition from his father) that the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) conducted nineteen military campaigns and he (actually) fought in eight of them"
4696,"It has been narrated by Buraida who heard it from his father that he joined the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) in sixteen military campaigns"
4697,"It has been narrated on the authority of Salama who said:I joined seven military expeditions led by the Messenger of Allah himself (ﷺ)"
4698,"The (foregoing) tradition has also been narrated on the authority of Hatim through the same chain of transmitters with the difference that according to this version both these types of expeditions were seven in number"
4699,"It has been narrated on the authority of Abu Musa (Ash'ari) who said:We set out on an expedition with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ). We were six in number and had (with us) only one camel which we rode turn by turn Our feet were injured. My feet were so badly injured that my nails dropped off. We covered our feet with rags. so this expedition was called Dhat-ur-Riqa' (i. e. the expedition of rags) because we bandaged our feet with rags (on that day). Abu Burda said: Abu Musa narrated this tradition"
4700,"It has been narrated on the authority of A'isha, wife of the Prophet ( صلی اللہ علیہ وسلم ), who said:
The Messenger of Allah ( صلی اللہ علیہ وسلم ) set out for Badr. When he reached Harrat-ul-Wabara (a place four miles from Medina) a man met him who was known for his valour and courage. The Companions of the Messenger of Allah ( صلی اللہ علیہ وسلم ) were pleased to see him. He said: I have come so that I may follow you and get a share from the booty. The Messenger of Allah ( صلی اللہ علیہ وسلم ) said to him: Do you believe in Allah and His Apostle? He said: No. The Messenger of Allah ( صلی اللہ علیہ وسلم ) said: Go back, I will not seek help from a Mushrik (polytheist). He went on until we reached Shajara, where the man met him again. He asked him the same question again and the man gave him the same answer. He said: Go back. Im will not seek help from a Mushrik. The man returned and overtook him at Baida'? He asked him as he had asked previously: Do you believe in Allah and His Apostle? The man said: Yes. The Messenger of Allah ( صلی اللہ علیہ"
